The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
while (i--) {
    liquidio_destroy_nic_device(octeon_dev, i);
}
```
Reason:
The `while (i--)` loop decrements `i` before entering the loop body. When a failure occurs at index `i`, the cleanup loop starts with `i` already decremented (e.g., from 3 to 2), so the device at the failing index `i` is never cleaned up, causing a memory leak.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
do {
    liquidio_destroy_nic_device(octeon_dev, i);
} while (i--);
```
Reason:
The `do { ... } while (i--)` loop first executes the body with the current `i` (the failing index), then decrements `i`. This ensures the device at the failing index is properly cleaned up before moving to lower indices.

